      "title": "GSEF 2026: Somalia's Ambassador to Kenya Says Somali Investors Are Tried and Tested - Dawan Africa",
      "link": "https://www.dawan.africa/news/gsef-2026-somalias-ambassador-to-kenya-says-somali-investors-are-tried-and-tested",
      "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 20:31:50 GMT",
      "image": "https://www.dawan.africa/news-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Futfs.io%2Ff%2Fv9D9ylrdiyZgpC7RMRXPyCTtNEgdIuYL79UkZbR0H23VF8oG",
      "content": "Sunday, October 25, 2026 Search Home Politics Business Opinion Sports Explainers Cryptocurrency Culture & Society Features News Tech Podcasts Videos Blockchain Somalia Kenya Djibouti Ethiopia Eritrea Somalia Kenya Djibouti Ethiopia Eritrea Loading article content... Uncovering the Continent — Through Its Own Lens 21 October Street, 405 Suldan Business Park, Mogadishu, Somalia +252628881171 Info@dawan.africa Quick Links Home Latest News About Us Categories Culture & Society Features Cryptocurrency Tech Blockchain Blockchain Recent Posts Somali President Meets Puntland Traditional Elder Amid Political Disputes. Oct 24, 2026 GSEF 2026: Somalia's Ambassador to Kenya Says Somali Investors Are Tried and Tested Oct 24, 2026 CS Aden Duale Jokes Somalia Has Many Gachaguas At GSEF 2026 Event in Nairobi Oct 24, 2026 Stay Updated with African News Get the latest breaking news and analysis delivered to your inbox. Subscribe Join our community of informed readers. Unsubscribe anytime. © 2026 Dawan Africa. All rights reserved. Privacy Policy Terms of Service Search GSEF 2026: Somalia's Ambassador to Kenya Says Somali Investors Are Tried and Tested | Dawan Africa | Dawan Africa GSEF 2026: Somalia's Ambassador to Kenya Says Somali Investors Are Tried and Tested October 24, 2026 5 min read HW By - Editor Hassan Wekesa Kenya, 24 October 2026 - Somalia’s Ambassador to Kenya, Jabril Ibrahim Abdulle, has hailed the Global Somali Entrepreneurship Forum (GSEF) as a platform that connects investors and businesses across the region, noting that trade improvements are evident. Speaking at the forum, now in its third year, he said remittances from the diaspora should go beyond consumption and be channelled into investment ventures back home, in the East Africa region and elsewhere where business environment is conducive. He noted that the forum continues to bring together investors, and that the eight member states of the East African Community (EAC) have a combined population of around 300 million people. The EAC member states are Kenya, Tanzania, Uganda, Burundi, Democratic Republic of the Congo, Rwanda, South Sudan, and Somalia. “Trade improvements are evident,” Ambassador Abdulle said at the Radisson Blu Hotel, Upper Hill, Nairobi, adding that networks connect businesses. He urged the Somali diaspora to keep working hard and not to worry about how long they have stayed away from home. Abdelaziz Adan Abdullahi explains to attendees of the Global Somali Entrepreneurship Forum (GSEF) 2026 how BananaPay works. Photo: Dawan Africa To him, what counts is the remittances that enable those back home to invest in various ventures rather than simply consume. “A man is judged by what he or she brings home, not necessarily how long he or she has stayed away,” Ambassador Abdulle said. He said the forum comes hot on the heels of both Kenyan and Somali leaders attending the 25th Common Market for Eastern and Southern Africa (COMESA) Summit of Heads of State and Government in Harare, Zimbabwe. He added that Kenya and Somalia share a lot in common, and that the relationship between the two neighbouring countries remains strong and warm. Ambassador Abdulle also said that envoys from many countries ask him how they can attract Somali investors, because “our people have been tried and tested.” Entrepreneurs Still Face Challenges Other speakers at GSEF 2026 said that while the Somali community is known to thrive in entrepreneurial ventures, both back home and in the diaspora, many businesses still encounter enormous challenges. Dr Ahmednur Abdi, the Chief Policy Advisor and former Permanent Secretary in the Office of the Prime Minister of the Federal Republic of Somalia, said entrepreneurs need simpler regulations and simpler dispute resolution mechanisms, among other interventions. He noted that, without any iota of doubt, the Somali people are resilient. “We should move from survival to reach potential. Somali innovation is visible,” he said. Dr Abdi urged both governments and investors to improve services and infrastructure. “We must ensure that the technologies we have help farmers, for instance, to reach markets,” he stated. Delegates follow proceedings during the Global Somali Entrepreneurship Forum (GSEF) 2026 at the Radisson Blu Hotel, Upper Hill, Nairobi, on Saturday, 24 October 2026. Photo: Dawan Africa He also called for collaboration between countries, their citizens and businesspeople so that they can tackle serious questions such as “can goods cross boundaries without delay?” Targeted investments in roads, energy and logistics, he said, would help streamline thriving business activity in the East African region. He urged governments in EAC member states to provide clear regulations. More from Kenya Somali President Meets Puntland Traditional Elder Amid Political Disputes. CS Aden Duale Jokes Somalia Has Many Gachaguas At GSEF 2026 Event in Nairobi However, Dr Abdi challenged the Federal Government of Somalia to wake up from its slumber, arguing that the private sector in Somalia was doing much better and that “the Government must keep at par with the private sector.” Dr Abdi is a seasoned governance and policy expert with more than 25 years of experience. He was glad to bring to the forum his deep expertise in public-sector reform, federalism, institutional development and lessons from post-conflict recovery. He established Somalia’s first Directors General Forum and has led the Federal Negotiation Technical Committee (FNTC), which brings together representatives of the Federal Government of Somalia (FGS) and the Federal Member States (FMS) to support negotiations on key federalism matters. He later led the State Formation Committee for the Northwest Somalia State. Duale: Somalia Must Find Political Goodwill The Chief Guest at the forum was Kenya’s Health Cabinet Secretary, Aden Duale, who said that people who do not know Somali business acumen and agility “take us for granted.” He said the time was ripe for Somalis back in the Federal Republic of Somalia to shape the destiny of their nation, because what is lacking there is political goodwill. “Countries that went under at the same time as Somalia are back on their feet. It is sad that 30 years later, Somalia still faces the same political fragility,” he said. He said many politicians in Somalia are his friends, but they must start thinking about how the country can turn around instead of burying their heads in the sand when it is supposed to sprout and grow. Health Cabinet Secretary Aden Duale addresses delegates as Chief Guest at the Global Somali Entrepreneurship Forum 2026 on Saturday, 24 October 2026. Photo: Dawan Africa He thanked the GSEF secretariat for offering the platform to investors, innovators and policymakers, among other stakeholders. He said that what counts now is for the platform to translate into practical opportunities, where entrepreneurs meet clients and strike meaningful business deals. “What matters now is not how many people attend this forum, but the business deals entrepreneurs get out of this distinguished gathering,” CS Duale stated. He said East Africa is at a critical moment, with countries still grappling with youth unemployment, poor infrastructure and other bottlenecks that bedevil the region. “The potential of our people can be tapped to realise full potential. We must create stronger pathways for entrepreneurs,” CS Duale said. He advised Somalia to follow in the footsteps of other EAC member states by ratifying all the requirements of the bloc, not just through the Legislative arm. He cited customs as an area the government should streamline if “Somalia wants to experience the business boom we see every day at the Kenya Ports Authority (KPA).” CS Duale said Kenya and Somalia have a strong connection, and that it is therefore important for both countries to enable entrepreneurs to access larger markets. “Government leadership is very important,” he advised. Businesses showcasing at the forum included Takaful Insurance, Beba Fleet, Fly Dubai, Amanah Insurance, BananaPay, Astral Aviation and Roasting Peacock. Abdelaziz Adan Abdullahi explains to attendees of the Global Somali Entrepreneurship Forum (GSEF) 2026 how BananaPay works. Photo: Dawan Africa Also present were Vickers Security Services Ltd, Aga Khan University, Aga Khan Hospital, Salaam Somali Bank, Travel Takaful, PayOn Time and Silva Gigiri. The two-day forum started on Saturday and ends on Sunday. Discover more Kenya updates Somalia’s Electoral Commission Announces Candidates for Hirshabelle State Leader and Deputy Meru launches formal review of its city-status credentials Ad Ad Like ( 0 ) Save ( 0 ) Share More Kenya Stories Back to Top More from Kenya Salaam Somali Bank Opens New Branch Building at Suuqbacaad Market Oct 24, 2026 News Business Read more → Amal Bank, Arafat Company Launch Hajj and Umrah Financing Service in Bosaso Oct 24, 2026 News Business Read more → Development Bank of Ethiopia Plans Shift to Indirect Lending Model Oct 24, 2026 News Business Read more →",
      "summary": "At the Global Somali Entrepreneurship Forum (GSEF) 2026 in Nairobi, Somalia's Ambassador to Kenya, Jabril Ibrahim Abdulle, emphasized the resilience and potential of Somali investors, highlighting their ability to contribute significantly to economic growth. He urged the Somali diaspora to invest in their homeland rather than merely sending remittances for consumption. Abdulle noted the importance of the East African Community (EAC), which comprises around 300 million people, in fostering trade and business connections. Despite the entrepreneurial spirit within the Somali community, challenges persist, as pointed out by Dr. Ahmednur Abdi, who called for simpler regulations and improved infrastructure to facilitate business operations. Kenyan Health Cabinet Secretary Aden Duale echoed these sentiments, stressing the need for political goodwill in Somalia to harness its economic potential. The forum showcased various businesses and aimed to create opportunities for meaningful partnerships among entrepreneurs, policymakers, and investors, ultimately seeking to enhance the region's economic landscape.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/gsef-2026-somalia-s-ambassador-to-kenya-says-somali-investor-2925c67b46.html"
    },
