      "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 19:54:47 GMT",
      "image": "https://ftlsomalia.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Hirshabelle-Leadership.jpg",
      "content": "Federal and Regional Leaders Congratulate Guudlaawe on Hirshabelle Re-election - Somali News today Live Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Election Hirshabelle Federal and Regional Leaders Congratulate Guudlaawe on Hirshabelle Re-election October 24, 2026 6 min read JOWHAR , Ali Abdullahi Hussein Guudlaawe has been re-elected as President of Hirshabelle State, securing 81 votes in Saturday’s parliamentary ballot in Jowhar, prompting a wave of congratulatory messages from federal and regional leaders across Somalia. Guudlaawe defeated Nur Ilmi Halane of the Ramaas party, who received 13 votes, with four ballots declared invalid. Lawmakers also elected Abdifatah Hassan Afrah, a former Hiiraan governor, as Vice President. Both ran under the Justice and Solidarity Party (JSP), which holds 78 of the 99 seats in the regional assembly following the September 30 direct elections. Federal Leadership Welcomes Result President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud was among the first to congratulate Guudlaawe and Afrah, wishing them success in carrying out their responsibilities. He called for accelerated efforts to strengthen peace, reconciliation, state-building, and public services across Hirshabelle, and commended the electoral process, stressing the importance of advancing democratisation and encouraging public participation in Somalia’s political process. Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre also extended congratulations, urging the new leadership to prioritise peace and security, social cohesion, reconciliation, and improved public services. Speaker of the House of the People Abdulkadir Mohamed Nur (Jaamac) welcomed the results in a detailed statement. “I congratulate the new leader of the Hirshabelle Regional State, Mr. Ali Abdullahi Hussein (Guudlaawe), and his deputy, Mr. Abdifatah Hassan Afrah, who today won the election held in Jowhar,” he said. “I commend the lawmakers, the leadership of the House of Representatives, the candidates who competed, and the people of Hirshabelle for demonstrating political understanding, diligence, patience, and civic awareness throughout the electoral process. This reflects the progress of the democratic process and the confirmation of the will of the Somali people.” He prayed for the new leadership to succeed in their entrusted responsibilities and to lead Hirshabelle toward peace, unity, and development, as reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of federal reactions to the Hirshabelle process . Interior and Federal Affairs Minister Ali Yusuf Ali (Hosh) also sent his congratulations. “I extend my congratulations and commendation for the election of the leader of the Hirshabelle regional government, Ali Abdullahi Hussein (Guudlaawe), and the Vice President, Abdifatah Hassan Afrah,” he said in a post on X. Parliamentary and Regional Messages Sadia Yasin Samatar, First Deputy Speaker of the House of the People, sent a message of congratulations to Guudlaawe and Afrah, praying that God would guide them in their responsibilities and support them in serving the community and advancing Hirshabelle’s development. She expressed hope for a leadership built on diligence, honesty, and a developmental vision, working for peace, unity, and the progress of the people of Hirshabelle. Former Prime Minister Ali Mohamed Gedi also congratulated Guudlaawe, describing him as an experienced and moderate leader who would bring peace, stability, and sustainable development to Hirshabelle. Gedi extended his congratulations to Vice President Afrah as well, wishing both success in their entrusted duties. South West State President Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed (Laftagareen), Galmudug President-elect Liban Ahmed Hassan, and other regional figures also sent congratulatory messages. Liban highlighted the longstanding ties between Hirshabelle and Galmudug and called for closer cooperation between the neighbouring states. The Hiiraan Dispute Remains Unresolved While the congratulations reflect a broad political consensus around the outcome, the election itself was not without controversy. The JSP’s decision to renominate Guudlaawe, an Abgaal from Middle Shabelle, deepened a long-running dispute with the Hawadle community of Hiiraan, who argue that the presidency should rotate to their region under the power-sharing formula that created Hirshabelle in 2016. Outgoing Vice President Yusuf Ahmed Hagar Dabageed, a Hawadle politician, rejected the Jowhar process, stating before the vote that “any result from that sham does not apply to Hiiraan.” He accused President Mohamud and his allies of proceeding without a political or community agreement. The JSP’s selection of Afrah, a former Hiiraan governor, as the vice-presidential candidate was intended to balance the ticket and address Hawadle concerns. But the arrangement does not satisfy the community’s demand for the presidency itself. What Comes Next Guudlaawe’s new administration will inherit a state with persistent security challenges, including Al-Shabaab activity in rural areas, and unresolved political tensions that have periodically disrupted governance in Beledweyne. The Hawadle community’s sense of exclusion, articulated by the outgoing vice president and supported by traditional elders, will not disappear because the parliamentary arithmetic favors the JSP. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the election , the opposing candidates from Ramaas and Iftiin Qaran were widely viewed as a malxiis , a token opposition serving to provide a veneer of democratic competition for a contest whose outcome was already decided by the JSP’s two-thirds majority. Critical Note The congratulations from Villa Somalia, the federal parliament, and regional leaders are standard political courtesies, but they carry a particular weight in the Hirshabelle context. The federal government has invested considerable political capital in the JSP’s regional strategy, and Guudlaawe’s re-election represents a consolidation of that strategy following the party’s earlier success in Galmudug. But the praise for the “electoral process” and the “political maturity” of Hirshabelle’s lawmakers cannot obscure the unresolved political tensions in the region. The JSP’s 78-seat majority in the regional assembly means that Guudlaawe was elected by a chamber that the ruling party dominates, in a vote that the outgoing vice president publicly rejected as a “sham.” The Hawadle community’s demand for the presidency remains unaddressed, and the selection of a former Hiiraan governor as the JSP’s vice-presidential candidate has not resolved the underlying dispute over power-sharing. Guudlaawe will govern with a parliament that supports him and a federal government that sponsors him. Whether he can govern with the consent of Hiiraan’s political establishment is a different question. The answer will determine whether Hirshabelle’s next administration is stable or simply another chapter in the cycle of contested leadership that has defined the state since its formation. Recommended Reading On ftlsomalia.com Guudlaawe Faces a Malxiis Opposition as Hirshabelle Presidential Election Proceeds Hirshabelle Parliament to Elect Speaker and President in Coming Days Villa Somalia and Federal Parliament Praise New Hirshabelle Speakership Hirshabelle Election Results: JSP Wins Landslide in First Direct Vote Gudlawe Replaces Hiiraan Governor with Former Officeholder as Regional Election Looms Facebook X Email WhatsApp LinkedIn Guudlaawe Faces a Malxiis Opposition as Hirshabelle Presidential Election Proceeds You may also like Election Guudlaawe Faces a Malxiis Opposition as Hirshabelle Presidential Election Proceeds October 24, 2026 4 min read Election Villa Somalia and Federal Parliament Praise New Hirshabelle Speakership October 23, 2026 4 min read Election PM Hamza Abdi Barre Praises Galmudug Lawmakers as Liban Hassan and Garyare Take Office October 22, 2026 4 min read Latest News Somalia’s Danab Commandos Launch Operation Banooda, Kill Seven Al-Shabaab Militants Federal and Regional Leaders Congratulate Guudlaawe on Hirshabelle Re-election Mogadishu Hosts Uganda’s 64th Independence Anniversary After Landmark Trade Talks Ismail Mohamed Shines as Somalia’s U-15 Cubs Win FIFA Opener Against Puerto Rico Min. Fiqi Thanks EU Amb. Máthé for Security Support as Mogadishu Pushes for Faster International Cooperation Deadly Oromo-Somali Clashes in Moyale Kill Nine, Displace Thousands to Kenya Parliament Moves to Question Health Minister as 20 MPs Submit No-Confidence Motion Guudlaawe Faces a Malxiis Opposition as Hirshabelle Presidential Election Proceeds Addis Ababa and Asmara Trade War Accusations as Moscow Prepares to Host Talks Nairobi Police Hunt Building Owner as Looting Disrupts Eastleigh Rescue Efforts Facility for Talo and Leadership. Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. ABOUT US | PRIVACY POLICY Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia",
      "summary": "Ali Abdullahi Hussein Guudlaawe has been re-elected as President of Hirshabelle State, receiving 81 votes in a parliamentary election held in Jowhar. His opponent, Nur Ilmi Halane, garnered only 13 votes, with four ballots invalidated. Guudlaawe's victory prompted congratulations from various federal and regional leaders, including President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud and Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre, who emphasized the need for peace, reconciliation, and effective governance. Abdifatah Hassan Afrah was elected as Vice President, both representing the Justice and Solidarity Party (JSP), which holds a significant majority in the assembly. However, the election has sparked controversy, particularly among the Hawadle community, who feel excluded from power-sharing agreements. Despite the JSP's dominance, unresolved tensions may challenge Guudlaawe's administration, particularly regarding the demands of the Hiiraan region for a rotational presidency. The new leadership faces ongoing security issues and political disputes that could impact stability in Hirshabelle.",
