      "title": "Ethiopia-style guerrilla insurgencies could emerge in Somalia: official - Somali Guardian",
      "link": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/ethiopia-style-guerrilla-insurgencies-somalia/",
      "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:29:24 GMT",
      "image": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Mogadishu.jpg",
      "content": "Ethiopia-style guerrilla insurgencies could emerge in Somalia: official | Somali Guardian Facebook Instagram X VKontakte Youtube News Somalia Africa World Featured Editor Picks Editorials Search 26.3 C Mogadishu News Somalia Africa World Featured Editor Picks Editorials Privacy Policy Sign in Welcome! Log into your account your username your password Forgot your password? Get help Password recovery Recover your password your email A password will be e-mailed to you. Facebook Instagram X VKontakte Youtube Somali Guardian News Somalia Africa World Featured Editor Picks Editorials Home News Somalia Ethiopia-style guerrilla insurgencies could emerge in Somalia: official Ethiopia-style guerrilla insurgencies could emerge in Somalia: official October 24, 2026 Share Facebook X Pinterest WhatsApp MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Somalia could face the emergence of Ethiopia-style guerrilla factions in several regions as political rivalries deepen and competing power centres reportedly seek military backing, former Somali intelligence chief Abdirahman Turyare warned on Friday. Turyare, who currently serves as Somalia’s envoy to the Islamic Military Counter-Terrorism Coalition, cautioned that armed movements resembling those operating in Ethiopia’s Tigray, Amhara and Oromia regions could emerge in Somalia, particularly in Hiran, Middle Shabelle and Bay. He warned that the potential factions could eventually forge alliances with Al-Shabaab, the Al-Qaeda-linked militant group waging an insurgency against Somalia’s federal government, and urged authorities to prevent their emergence before they develop into a broader security threat. Turyare also issued a stark warning to those allegedly seeking to establish the armed factions, cautioning them against gambling with their lives by pursuing a course that could plunge the country into further violence. His remarks come amid growing reports in Somali local media suggesting that political disputes between Mogadishu and regional power brokers are increasingly intersecting with allegations of external military support and preparations for new armed formations. Sanbalolshe reportedly seeks military backing in Ethiopia At the centre of the latest concerns is former Somali intelligence chief Abdullahi Mohamed Ali Sanbalolshe, who, according to local media reports, is in the border town of Ferfer to receive military support from Ethiopia. The reports allege that Sanbalolshe intends to establish a powerful armed force capable of wresting control of the entire Hiran region from the federal government. His reported plans come against the backdrop of a growing political dispute involving his Hawadle clan and Mogadishu over the leadership of the Hirshabelle regional administration. The disagreement centres on the selection of the region’s president. Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud backed Ali Gudlawe, who belongs to his own clan, for the position. Gudlawe was re-elected on Saturday, further intensifying the political tensions surrounding the regional leadership contest. The allegations concerning Sanbalolshe’s movements, military support and reported plans have not been independently verified. Middle Shabelle tensions and alleged UAE support A separate set of local media reports points to possible preparations for another armed faction in neighbouring Middle Shabelle. Abdullahi Mohamed Nur, a wealthy businessman affiliated with the clan of opposition leader Sharif Sheikh Ahmed, is reportedly planning to establish a guerrilla force in the region, allegedly with military support from the United Arab Emirates. The reports further claim that Nur possesses a substantial number of Ma’awisley fighters drawn from his own clan. Ma’awisley forces are locally organised armed groups that have played a significant role in Somalia’s fight against Al-Shabaab, particularly in central regions. Their potential mobilisation amid political disputes could add another layer of complexity to an already fragile security environment. The allegations of planned military assistance from the UAE, like those concerning Ethiopia, remain unverified. Bay remains a flashpoint after Laftagareen’s ouster The warnings come as Somalia’s federal government continues to grapple with guerrilla warfare by forces loyal to former Southwest State leader Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen. Laftagareen was ousted from power in March by a military operation, forcing his forces out of Baidoa, the interim regional capital. Since then, his fighters have shifted to guerrilla tactics, challenging federal authority across the region. According to the reports, Laftagareen’s forces made significant territorial gains last week, retaking five districts across Bay region, leaving Baidoa as the only town in the region outside their control. The renewed fighting underscores the difficulties facing the federal government as it attempts to consolidate its authority following the change of leadership in Southwest State. The developments also raise concerns that political confrontations, if increasingly pursued through armed proxies and irregular forces, could open additional fronts in a country already fighting a protracted insurgency. Fears of a wider security crisis The Somali federal government has not yet publicly commented on the reports concerning the alleged formation of new guerrilla factions, the purported external military backing or Turyare’s warning. The reports have nevertheless emerged amid mounting political tensions across Somalia, fuelling concerns that disputes between Mogadishu and its opponents could escalate into a wider cycle of armed confrontation. The potential consequences extend beyond individual regional power struggles. The emergence of additional armed factions in Hiran, Middle Shabelle and Bay could complicate counterterrorism operations, weaken federal authority and create opportunities for Al-Shabaab to exploit divisions among political and clan-based rivals. Turyare’s warning highlights the risk that armed groups established to advance political or regional interests could eventually become part of a more fragmented security landscape, particularly if they develop relationships with established militant organisations. For Somalia, the prospect of simultaneous conflicts involving federal forces, regional factions, clan-based militias and al-Shabaab presents a serious challenge at a time when the country continues to depend on extensive international political, financial and security support. Somalia has spent years rebuilding state institutions and security structures following decades of conflict and state collapse. A further spread of politically driven armed violence could undermine those gains, deepen regional instability and threaten the fragile process of state-building that has continued since 2004. Contact us: info@somaliguardian.com Share Facebook X Pinterest WhatsApp Previous article Somalia sends more than 200 military vehicles toward Burhakabo Somaliguardian news RELATED ARTICLES MORE FROM AUTHOR Somalia Somalia sends more than 200 military vehicles toward Burhakabo Somalia Somalia opposition scores own goal over Galmudug election Somalia Somalia: Key Laftagaren ally survives alleged Turkish drone strike Somalia Somalia’s opposition launches first court challenge to new constitution Somalia Somalia parliament speaker accuses Kenya of harbouring terrorists Somalia Somalia angry as Ethiopia refuses to intervene in South West State conflict Somalia Somalia police enter Hormuud headquarters in Mogadishu amid Southwest state tensions Somalia Somalia president rejects opposition calls for consensus-based election Somalia Somalia’s government loses all major Bay towns but Baidoa to Laftagaren Somalia Somalia’s government loses Burhakabo to ousted Southwest leader’s forces Somalia Somalia agrees to buy Pakistani JF-17 fighters Somalia Somalia says 260 armed men removed from opposition leaders’ homes ABOUT US Somaliguardian is an East African leading news website. We are impartial and independent, every day we create distinctive, world class content which inform and educate thousands of people in Somalia, East Africa and around the world. Get updated with our real time news coverage from Somalia, Africa and the entire world. FOLLOW US Facebook Instagram X VKontakte Youtube News Somalia Africa World Featured Editor Picks Editorials Privacy Policy © 2021 Somaliguardian error: Content is protected!",
      "summary": "Former Somali intelligence chief Abdirahman Turyare has warned that Somalia may face the emergence of guerrilla insurgencies similar to those in Ethiopia, particularly in regions like Hiran, Middle Shabelle, and Bay. Turyare, now Somalia’s envoy to the Islamic Military Counter-Terrorism Coalition, expressed concerns that political rivalries and external military support could lead to the formation of armed factions that might align with Al-Shabaab, the militant group opposing the federal government. Reports indicate that former intelligence chief Abdullahi Mohamed Ali Sanbalolshe is seeking military backing from Ethiopia to establish a force in Hiran amid political disputes over regional leadership. Additionally, businessman Abdullahi Mohamed Nur is allegedly planning to form a guerrilla group in Middle Shabelle with support from the UAE. The situation is exacerbated by ongoing guerrilla warfare in Bay, where forces loyal to ousted leader Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen have gained territory. Turyare's warnings highlight the potential for escalating violence and instability in Somalia.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/ethiopia-style-guerrilla-insurgencies-could-emerge-in-somali-d91b5b773a.html"
